    "slug": "pentagon-report-153-civilians-killed-in-us-airstrikes-in-yemen-in-2025",
    "summary": "A newly released Pentagon assessment confirms that US airstrikes in Yemen in 2025 resulted in 153 civilian deaths and 243 injuries, all from three strikes in April. The report marks a rare acknowledgment of civilian harm in the US campaign against Houthi targets.",
    "content": "The US Department of Defense has released an assessment indicating that American airstrikes conducted in Yemen during 2025 caused 153 civilian deaths and wounded 243 others. The report, which was made public without classification, states that all civilian casualties recorded for the year stemmed from three separate strikes that occurred in April 2025.\n\nThe strikes targeted Houthi positions and nearby residential areas in and around the capital, Sanaa, as well as the oil port of Ras Isa. The Pentagon's report provides a detailed account of the human toll of the military campaign, which has been ongoing as part of US efforts to counter Houthi threats to international shipping and regional stability.\n\nThis disclosure is significant because it offers an official count of civilian harm, a topic that has often been disputed or opaque in conflict zones. The report’s release suggests an increased willingness by the US military to acknowledge unintended consequences of its operations, though it does not specify whether any disciplinary actions were taken or compensation offered to victims' families.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this news resonates deeply, as many in the community have experienced or witnessed the effects of similar airstrikes in Somalia, where US forces have conducted operations against militant groups. The Yemen report highlights the broader human costs of counterterrorism campaigns in the region, prompting questions about the accuracy of civilian casualty reporting in other theaters, including Somalia.\n\nThe findings also come at a time when regional tensions remain high, with Houthi forces continuing to launch attacks on commercial vessels. The US military has defended its operations as necessary for protecting freedom of navigation, but the civilian toll documented in this assessment is likely to fuel debates about the proportionality and precision of such strikes.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/08/13/duqeymaha-mareykanka-ee-yemen-oo-sababay-dhimashada-153-qof/",
