    "slug": "total-solar-eclipse-sweeps-across-parts-of-europe",
    "summary": "A total solar eclipse darkened skies over parts of Europe on Wednesday, drawing millions of observers. It was the first such event visible on the continent since 1999.",
    "content": "On Wednesday, August 13, 2026, a total solar eclipse crossed parts of Europe, plunging areas into darkness for nearly two minutes. The path of totality extended from the Arctic, over Greenland and Iceland, before reaching northern Spain and a small part of northeastern Portugal. Millions of people gathered to witness the rare celestial event.\n\nThe eclipse was the first total solar eclipse visible from the European continent since 1999. Totality, when the moon completely blocks the sun, lasted about two minutes in locations along the central path. In regions outside the path, a partial eclipse was observed, with the sun appearing as a crescent.\n\nAstronomers and local authorities had prepared for the event, providing safety guidance and organizing viewing events. Many people used specialized glasses to safely observe the phenomenon, while scientists used the opportunity to study the sun's corona and other atmospheric effects.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, many of whom have settled in European countries such as the Netherlands, the UK, and Scandinavia, this event served as a reminder of the wonders of nature that can be experienced in their adopted homes. It also highlighted the importance of scientific education and community gatherings, aspects that resonate with Somali cultural values of togetherness and learning.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/08/13/qorrax-madoobaad-buuxa-oo-yurub-qaybo-ka-mid-ah-mugdi-geliyay/",
